Contributing to TideSQL
=======================

When contributing to TideSQL, keep a couple things in mind:

  - Read RULES.md first. It is the short list of rules the engine is written
    to -- bounded loops, checked return values, no conditional compilation
    outside the compat layer, zero-warning builds -- and a change that breaks
    one of them will be sent back.
  - Make sure your code comments are all lower case and in English and
    using /* */ style. Comments explain why, not what the code already says.
  - Follow the existing MySQL storage engine coding conventions.
  - Format before you submit, with ./code_formatter.sh. It applies the
    .clang-format in the repository root; do not run clang-format without it,
    since the tool's own defaults do not match this codebase.
  - Assure your changes are tested and pass all tests. Build the plugin into a
    MySQL source tree as doc/01-getting-started/install.md describes, then from
    the build's mysql-test directory run both suites:

        ./mtr --suite=tidesdb,tidesdb_rpl --parallel=4 --force

    tidesdb_rpl is a separate suite and is not covered by --suite=tidesdb, so
    run both. If the linked TidesDB library was built against jemalloc,
    mimalloc or tcmalloc, preload the allocator or the plugin will not load at
    all -- install.md has the error and the fix.
  - Make sure your changes are well documented. A change to observable
    behaviour belongs in /doc, and a change to the public surface -- an option,
    a system or status variable, an engine capability -- belongs in
    VERSIONING.md as well.


Contributor License Agreement (CLA)
------------------------------------

To ensure TideSQL can be widely adopted and supported by individuals and
organizations, new contributions require agreement to a Contributor License
Agreement.

Individual contributors must agree to the TideSQL Individual Contributor
License Agreement (CLA). See the CLA file in this repository.

Corporate contributors -- if your employer or organization has rights to the
intellectual property you create (e.g., through an employment or contractor
agreement), your employer must execute the Corporate Contributor License
Agreement (CCLA) before your contributions can be accepted. See the CCLA file
in this repository, or contact cla@tidesdb.com to initiate the corporate
signing process.

  - Contributions made before January 31st, 2025 are governed solely by the
    Developer Certificate of Origin (DCO).

  - Contributions made on or after January 31st, 2025 require both:
      - A Signed-off-by commit (DCO), and
      - Agreement to the CLA (individual) or CCLA (corporate).

The CLA and CCLA do not change the open source license of TideSQL. All
contributions remain distributed under the GNU General Public License,
version 2 (GPLv2).


Developer Certificate of Origin
--------------------------------

By contributing to TideSQL, you certify that your contribution was created in
whole or in part by you, that you have the right to submit it under the GNU
General Public License version 2, and that you understand and agree that the
project and your contribution are public and that a record of the contribution
(including all personal information you submit with it) is maintained
indefinitely and may be redistributed consistent with the project and the
license.

All contributions must include a "Signed-off-by" line in the commit message.
This certifies that you agree to the Developer Certificate of Origin (see the
DCO file for the full text).

DCO sign-off is automatically checked by the DCO bot
(https://github.com/apps/dco) on all pull requests. The bot will show a status
check that must pass before your PR can be merged. Pull requests with commits
missing sign-off will be blocked until all commits are signed off.

To sign off your commits, use the -s or --signoff flag:

    git commit -s -m "Your commit message"

Or add the following line to your commit message:

    Signed-off-by: Your Name <your.email@example.com>

If you forgot to sign off on previous commits, you can amend them:

    git commit --amend -s

    # For multiple commits, use interactive rebase:
    git rebase -i HEAD~n  # where n is the number of commits
    # Mark commits as 'edit', then run:
    git commit --amend -s && git rebase --continue

You must use your real name and a valid email address. Pseudonyms or anonymous
contributions are not allowed.
